        "content": "{\n  \"rule\": \"Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and a new passage of about 250 words in the text field.\",\n  \"text\": \"The laundromat on Mercer Street had one machine nobody used.\\n\\nNumber nine. Brass buttons, cloudy glass, a coin slot sealed with solder.\\n\\nWhen I asked the attendant about it, she handed me a strip of masking tape.\\n\\n“Put your name on your basket,” she said. “Not your clothes.”\\n\\nI assumed she meant theft.\\n\\nAt eleven, while my sheets tumbled, number nine began to fill.\\n\\nInside was the yellow coat my brother had worn the day he disappeared. He was six. I was supposed to be watching him.\\n\\nI crossed the room.\\n\\nThe attendant caught my sleeve.\\n\\n“Let it finish.”\\n\\nThrough the glass, the coat turned slowly. One sleeve lifted against the drum, as though someone inside it was reaching for the door.\\n\\nI pulled away from her.\\n\\n“You don’t understand.”\\n\\n“Yes,” she said. “That’s why I work here.”\\n\\nThe machine stopped.\\n\\nIts door clicked open, and river water spread across the tiles.\\n\\nThe coat lay empty on the drum’s bottom. I knew the crooked stitches where Mum had repaired its pocket.\\n\\nFrom deep inside came my brother’s voice.\\n\\n“Have you found me yet?”\\n\\nI knelt in the water.\\n\\nThe attendant slapped masking tape across my mouth.\\n\\nSomething struck the drum from behind. Then again, harder.\\n\\nShe dragged me backward as small fingers curled around the open door.\\n\\nWe reached the street before I tore off the tape.\\n\\n“Why couldn’t I answer?”\\n\\nShe locked the entrance. Her hands were shaking.\\n\\n“Look at your basket.”\\n\\nMy name was gone from the tape.\\n\\nIn its place, written in my brother’s careful schoolboy letters, were the words YOU’RE IT.\\n\\nBehind the glass, every machine began to empty.\"\n}",
